WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.312
Here we make the NumPy-to-Chroma transition even more explicit.

00:00:04.312 --> 00:00:09.262
In the first version, we call a function such as dense_retrieve.

00:00:09.262 --> 00:00:14.272
In the second version, we call retrieve_chroma.

00:00:14.272 --> 00:00:21.862
After that, the retrieved rows still move into the same prompt builder and the same generation function.

00:00:21.862 --> 00:00:25.228
This is what we mean by a swappable retrieval layer.

00:00:25.889 --> 00:00:29.156
It also gives us a clean way to compare systems.

00:00:29.156 --> 00:00:37.171
We can run the same evaluation questions against NumPy, Chroma, Qdrant, or another backend and see whether the retrieved

00:00:37.171 --> 00:00:47.061
evidence changes. In a company, the retrieval interface may be an internal service rather than a Python function,

00:00:47.061 --> 00:00:49.836
but the architectural idea is the same.

00:00:49.836 --> 00:00:56.571
The application should depend on what retrieval returns—text, metadata, scores—not on the internal details of how the

00:00:56.571 --> 00:01:01.024
database performs nearest-neighbor search.
